# Platforms

`platforms/index.ts` loads `./${process.platform}` (`darwin` | `linux` | `win32`) and exports a singleton implementing `Platform`.

## Interface

```ts
addToTrustStores(certPath, options?)
removeFromTrustStores(certPath)
addDomainToHostFileIfMissing(domain)
deleteProtectedFiles / readProtectedFile / writeProtectedFile
```

## Per OS

| | Trust store | Firefox / Chrome | CA key protection |
|--|-------------|------------------|-------------------|
| **darwin** | `security add-trusted-cert` → System.keychain | NSS via Homebrew `nss` `certutil`, else Firefox wizard | root-owned `chmod 600` via sudo |
| **linux** | Debian: `/usr/local/share/ca-certificates` + `update-ca-certificates`; RHEL/Fedora (`update-ca-trust` present): `/etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors` + `update-ca-trust` | Firefox + Chrome `~/.pki/nssdb` via `certutil` (`apt`/`libnss3-tools` or `dnf`/`yum`/`nss-tools`); Chrome has no GUI fallback | same as darwin |
| **win32** | Windows `certutil -addstore -user root` | Always Firefox GUI wizard (no easy NSS certutil) | AES256 encrypt with user password (`createCipher`/`createDecipher`) |

## Shared (`shared.ts`)

- NSS DB discovery: `cert8.db` (legacy) / `cert9.db` (modern `sql:` prefix).
- `closeFirefox` before NSS writes (Firefox reloads DB on exit).
- `openCertificateInFirefox`: temp HTTP server hosts CA cert to trigger import wizard.
- `assertNotTouchingFiles`: only allow ops under config / legacy config dirs.

## Caveats when editing

- Linux Chrome without `certutil` cannot be fixed via wizard — warn via UI.
- Hosts edits on darwin/linux use `sudo tee -a`; Windows uses `sudo-prompt`.
- `removeCertificateFromNSSCertDB` currently re-adds with `-A` (likely bug; do not copy that pattern blindly).
